    "case_id": "459",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 60s presented with worsening dyspnea on exertion and pedal edema developing gradually over 2 months. She had no medical history and was only taking over-the-counter vitamin supplements. She was previously at her baseline functional status until 2 months ago when symptoms began progressing.\n\nHeart rate: 125 beats/minBlood pressure: 100/70 mm HgFindings:- Jugular venous distention- Bilateral pedal edema\n\n- ECG Studies:  Initial ECG: Narrow complex tachycardia (125 bpm), P wave preceding every QRS with 1:1 relationship, P-wave duration 80ms in lead II, positive P wave in lead II, negative in V1/V2, voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy, normal QRS precordial progression, asymmetrical T-wave inversions in lateral precordial leads  Follow-up ECG: Heart rate 100 bpm, P-wave duration 120ms in lead II, positive P wave in inferior leads, biphasic in V1/V2- Laboratory Tests:  Brain-type natriuretic peptide: 1700 pg/mL- Imaging Studies:  Transthoracic echocardiogram: Severe left ventricular systolic dysfunction, ejection fraction 15-20%, grade 2 diastolic dysfunction  Coronary angiography: No significant coronary disease  - Telemetry Findings:  Abrupt changes in heart rates while at rest, lasting few minutes  Premature atrial complex preceding onset of arrhythmia",
